@@ -0,0 +1,95 @@
+# USDC Pay
+
+A one-page dApp for sending USDC on Ethereum mainnet. Connect a wallet, enter a
+recipient and an amount, send. The page shows your USDC balance (what you can
+send) and your ETH balance (what pays the gas).
+
+Token: [`0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48`](https://etherscan.io/address/0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48) — Circle USDC, **6 decimals**.
+
+## Stack
+
+- **Next.js 15** (App Router, TypeScript) — SSR shell, one client-rendered route
+- **wagmi 2 + viem 2** — chain reads/writes, typed ABIs, precise error surfaces
+- **RainbowKit 2** — wallet discovery and connection UI
+- **TanStack Query** — request caching for every chain read
+- **Tailwind CSS 4**
+
+## Running it
+
+```bash
+npm install
+cp .env.example .env.local   # optional, see below
+npm run dev                  # http://localhost:3000/pay
+```
+
+| Variable | Needed for |
+| --- | --- |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID` | WalletConnect / mobile wallets. Browser-extension wallets work without it. |
+| `NEXT_PUBLIC_MAINNET_RPC_URL` | Your own mainnet RPC. Without it, viem's public fallback is used — fine for local dev, rate-limited for production. |
+
+```bash
+npm run build      # production build
+npm test           # unit tests for the money-handling logic
+npm run lint
+npm run typecheck
+```
+
+## How the payment works
+
+`USDC.transfer(recipient, amount)` from the user's own wallet. No approvals, no
+intermediary contract, no server: the app never holds funds or keys.
+
+The flow is deliberately two-step — **Review payment** then **Confirm** — because
+an ERC-20 transfer cannot be undone.
+
+## Details worth knowing
+
+**Decimals.** USDC has 6, not 18. Amounts are parsed with `parseUnits(input, decimals)`
+on the string the user typed; nothing is ever routed through a JavaScript float,
+where `0.1 * 1e6` is not an integer. `decimals()` is read from the contract at
+runtime and the constant `6` is only a pre-fetch fallback.
+
+**Simulation before signing.** Every send is dry-run with `eth_call` first. USDC
+is a pausable, blacklistable, upgradeable token, so a transfer can fail for
+reasons a balance check cannot predict. Simulating catches a paused contract, a
+blacklisted sender or recipient, and a balance that moved — before the wallet
+prompt, instead of as a failed transaction the user still pays for.
+
+**Gas.** The estimated fee (`gas × maxFeePerGas`) is shown on the review screen
+and compared with the ETH balance. Zero ETH gets a clear warning up front — it
+is the most common reason an otherwise valid USDC payment cannot be sent.
+
+**Recipients.** Accepts a hex address or an ENS name, and resolves both ways so a
+pasted address shows its ENS name back to you. Blocked: malformed addresses, the
+zero address, and the USDC contract itself. Warned: your own address, and
+contract addresses that may not be able to move tokens out again. EIP-7702
+delegated EOAs (`0xef0100…` code) are *not* treated as contracts — they are
+ordinary wallets, and warning on them would train users to ignore the warning.
+
+**Balances** refresh on every new block and immediately after a confirmed
+transfer, and are displayed at full precision with tabular figures.
+
+**Errors** are translated from viem's error chain into one actionable sentence
+each (`transfer amount exceeds balance` → "Not enough USDC in your wallet"), with
+the raw reason kept for support. A wallet rejection is shown as information, not
+as a failure.
+
+## Layout
+
+```
+src/
+  app/pay/page.tsx        the route
+  components/PayForm.tsx  form → review → confirm → status
+  components/Balances.tsx USDC and ETH tiles
+  hooks/                  balances, recipient resolution, gas estimation
+  lib/amount.ts           string ⇄ base-unit conversion (unit-tested)
+  lib/errors.ts           viem errors → human copy (unit-tested)
+  lib/usdc.ts             address + ABI
+  lib/wagmi*.ts           chain, transport and connector config
+```
+
+## Before shipping
+
+- Set both env vars; the public RPC will rate-limit real traffic.
+- The two-step review is the only guard against a mistyped recipient. If you add
+  an address book or recent-recipients list, keep the review step.
